# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Choosing lines in a register and acting on them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A register is where the work gets done, so what can be done to a
\ line is reached from the line itself.
Details A selection is a set of lines. How it was made is the table's business
\ and not the program's: a click takes one, shift extends the run from
\ the line last clicked, and control adds or removes a single line -- so
\ a run and a scattered set are the same thing once they exist, and
\ nothing below needs to know which gesture was used.
\
\ Everything on the menu acts on the whole selection. Recategorising
\ twenty lines at once is the reason the selection is worth having: a
\ statement arrives with twenty charges from one shop, and categorising
\ them one at a time is the work the program is supposed to save.

Attributes RegisterSelect
Description The lines of the register that are selected, counting from one in
\ the order the register is showing.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Line      | Integer | 1       |

Attributes MenuItem
Description One item of the menu offered on a selection, in the order shown.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Item      | Text    |         |
| Enabled   | Boolean | true    |

Attributes CategoryChoice
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |

Attributes DeletionCount
Description How many transactions a delete actually removed, because a
\ selection of twenty is too many to count by eye.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Removed   | Integer | 0       |

Attributes Recategorised
Description What came of it, counted, because a selection of twenty is too many
\ to check by eye.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Changed   | Integer | 0       |
| Refused   | Integer | 0       |
| Reason    | String  | none    |

Scenario The menu on a selection offers what can be done to it
