Scenario The report and the register show the same transaction id
Description Shown in both places while the click-through is being checked: it
\ is how a line on one page is known to be the line on the other.
\ The id is a clock reading to the millisecond, so no table can write
\ it down -- what is checked is that it is there and that the two
\ agree. Expected to come off both pages once the navigation is
\ trusted; see the naming rule in Transactions for what it is.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
=FebruaryOnChecking
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy  | Category     |
| From     | 2026-02-01   |
| To       | 2026-02-28   |
| Interval | Whole        |
| Detail   | Transactions |
Then the transaction id shown is : IdShown
| Date       | Payee         | Blank | TheSame |
| 2026-02-01 | Shell         | false | true    |
| 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | false | true    |
| 2026-02-10 | Shell         | false | true    |
# Three transactions, three ids, and each one the same string in the report as
# in the register. Two transactions sharing an id would make the click-through
# land on the wrong line and is the thing this is really watching for.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The listings, and the menu on the tree
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Two windows that show the chart itself rather than the work done in
\ it -- one of the accounts, one of the categories -- and the menu
\ reached by right-clicking a line of the tree on the left.
Details The tree answers "where am I working". It groups the real accounts
\ under headings, totals each heading, leaves the hidden ones out until
\ they are asked for, and shows no categories at all. Every one of those
\ is right for working and wrong for maintaining: a category can only be
\ renamed if it can be found, and a hidden account can only be brought
\ back if it can be seen.
\
\ So each listing is one line per node, every node, in path order, with no
\ headings and nothing left out. The account listing carries the bank and
\ the number from the account's own dialog, because seeing them together
\ is how a statement is matched to the account it belongs to; the
\ category listing carries the alias, because that is the only place it
\ is ever read.
\
\ Both are windows of their own and not tabs. A tab is something to come
\ back to, and a listing is opened to find one node, change it, and close
\ again. Which menu they are on is not a table's business; that they are
\ reached from a menu rather than from the tree is, because the tree is
\ the thing they exist to get around.
\
\ Clicking a line opens the editor of the thing on that line, so finding
\ and changing are one gesture rather than two. The menu on the tree
\ opens the same editor, because there would be no sense in one account
\ having two settings windows depending on where it was clicked.

Attributes AccountListingRow
Description One line of the account listing, in path order so a parent reads
\ above its children.
| Attribute     | Type        | Default  |
| Path          | AccountPath |          |
| Type          | AccountType | Checking |
| Placeholder   | Boolean     | false    |
| Hidden        | Boolean     | false    |
| Bank          | Text        | none     |
| AccountNumber | Text        | none     |
| Balance       | Money       | 0.00     |

Attributes CategoryListingRow
Description One line of the category listing, in path order.
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| Path        | AccountPath |         |
| Type        | AccountType | Expense |
| Placeholder | Boolean     | false   |
| Hidden      | Boolean     | false   |
| Alias       | Text        | none    |

Attributes ListingRowSelect
Description The line clicked, named by the path on it.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Path      | AccountPath |         |

Attributes EditorOpened
Description Which editor came up and what it came up on. What the account
\ settings window holds is specified in Accounts; what is said here
\ is only that clicking a line opens it on the right node.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Window    | Text |         |
| Target    | Text | none    |

Attributes TreeNode
Description A line of the tree on the left, named the way the tree shows it --
\ a group heading by its heading, an account by its path.
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Node      | Text |         |

Scenario The account listing is every account, one line each
Description A placeholder is a line, a hidden account is a line, there are no
\ group headings, and the categories are not here at all -- they have
\ a listing of their own. Compare the account list scenarios above,
\ where each of those four is the other way round.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets             | Checking   | true        | false  |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Assets:Old Savings | Savings    | false       | true   |
| Liabilities:Visa   | CreditCard | false       | false  |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense    | false       | false  |
When account listing opened
Then account listing rows are : AccountListingRow CompareOnly
| Path               | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets             | Checking   | true        | false  |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Assets:Old Savings | Savings    | false       | true   |
| Liabilities:Visa   | CreditCard | false       | false  |
# Four lines for five rows of chart. Expenses:Groceries is a category and is in
# the other listing.

Scenario The account listing shows the bank and the number beside each account
Description The one place they are read together. An account with neither is
\ still a line, because a book where some accounts are labelled and
\ some are not is exactly when the listing is wanted.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Assets:Savings   | Savings    | false       | false  |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       | false  |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Assets:Checking |
| Bank          | First Bank      |
| AccountNumber | 0012-3456       |
When account settings changed : AccountSettings Vertical
| Path          | Liabilities:Visa |
| Bank          | First Bank       |
| AccountNumber | 4111             |
When account listing opened
Then account listing rows are : AccountListingRow CompareOnly
| Path             | Bank       | AccountNumber |
| Assets:Checking  | First Bank | 0012-3456     |
| Assets:Savings   | none       | none          |
| Liabilities:Visa | First Bank | 4111          |

Scenario The category listing is every category, which no other window shows
Description The tree shows none of these, so without this window a category
\ hidden by mistake cannot be found again, and one named badly
\ cannot be renamed.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       | false  |
| Expenses           | Expense  | true        | false  |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense  | true        | false  |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense  | false       | false  |
| Income:Salary      | Income   | false       | true   |
When category listing opened
