# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The width of a column
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Dragging the divider between two headings makes a column wider or
\ narrower. The width belongs to the column, is the same in every
\ register, and is kept with the book.
Details Every register is the same shape -- the same nine columns in the same
\ order, whatever the account -- so a width learned in one of them is
\ right in all of them. Widths held per account would mean widening the
\ payee column once per account, twenty times, for one preference about
\ how long a payee name is. Nobody would do that twice.
\
\ Kept with the book rather than with the program, for the same reason
\ every other setting is: a book is a file, and a book carried to
\ another machine should look the way it was left. See how a book is
\ stored in Books. The widths are one row per column there, keyed by the
\ column's name as this specification writes it -- not by its heading,
\ because Payment and Deposit are headed differently for different
\ account types and the width is not.
\
\ Only a column somebody resized is recorded. A column nobody touched
\ keeps whatever width the program gives it, and that number is not
\ stated anywhere here, so changing it does not fail a test. This is the
\ one thing that distinguishes "wide by default", which is appearance,
\ from "wide because I said so", which is not.

Attributes RegisterColumnWidth
Description The width of one register column, in pixels. A width is a number
\ only because a drag produces one; see the rule above for why no
\ default width appears in any table.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Column    | Text    |         |
| Width     | Integer | 0       |

BusinessRule A column cannot be dragged away to nothing
Description A width is clamped to a minimum. A column dragged to no width at
\ all would be invisible, and a column that cannot be seen cannot be
\ dragged back, so the program would have lost it until the book was
\ edited by hand.
Examples: WidthRequested
| Requested | Stored | Notes                                   |
| 240       | 240    | Taken as asked                          |
| 20        | 20     | The minimum exactly                     |
| 8         | 20     | Clamped; the divider stops here         |
| 0         | 20     | A drag past the next column stops here  |

Attributes WidthRequested
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Requested | Integer | 0       |
| Stored    | Integer | 0       |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

Scenario A column widened in one register is that wide in every register
Description The width is the column's, so moving to another account does not
\ undo it. Both of these registers have a payee column, and after
\ the drag there is one answer to how wide it is.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking  | Checking   | false       | false  |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path             |
| Liabilities:Visa |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column | Width |
| Payee  | 240   |

Scenario Only the columns somebody resized are recorded
Description The register has nine columns and one of them was dragged, so one
\ of them is written down. The other eight are whatever the program
\ makes them, which is deliberately not a number this
\ specification knows.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Memo |
| Width  | 180  |
Then recorded column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth
| Column | Width |
| Memo   | 180   |

Scenario A width survives the book being saved and opened again
Description A preference set once and lost on exit is worse than none, because
\ it has to be set again every time and it looks like a defect.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Payee |
| Width  | 240   |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | Category |
| Width  | 300      |
When the book is saved and opened again
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column   | Width |
| Payee    | 240   |
| Category | 300   |

Scenario A column dragged to nothing keeps the minimum width
Description The clamp from the rule above, through the window rather than in a
\ table of its own, because this is the case that would otherwise
\ lose a column for good.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type     | Placeholder | Hidden |
| Assets:Checking | Checking | false       | false  |
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path            |
| Assets:Checking |
When register column resized : RegisterColumnWidth Vertical
| Column | CheckNo |
| Width  | 0       |
Then register column widths are : RegisterColumnWidth CompareOnly
| Column  | Width |
| CheckNo | 20    |
